          "Understand and close the 7.4% demographic gap: the missing population is likely Hispanic/Latino, Native American, or recent immigrant communities. Genuine constituent engagement — not token outreach — in these communities is essential both for demographic accuracy and for preventing opposition mobilization around economic issues where these populations have real grievances.",
          "Attend specifically to the renter constituency: at 24.2%, renters represent roughly one-quarter of the district and are concentrated among younger adults and lower-income households. Housing costs, wage stagnation, and economic mobility for renters are real issues that existing Republican messaging may not address, and they are precisely the issues a Democratic challenger would target in building an opposition coalition.",
          "Do not treat 82-18 as a secure majority: this margin is built on a particular coalition of older, asset-holding, suburban homeowners. The demographic diversity (18.75% Hispanic, 7.4% gap, 24.2% renters) and the age-income intersection (aging on a middle income) create conditions where an opposition candidate who speaks credibly to economic anxiety and builds real community presence can erode enough Republican support to make this seat genuinely competitive in a future cycle."
