          { name: "Assuming Turnout Among Young Renters and Minority Communities Will Remain High", tag: "avoid", why: "The 69% Democratic result depends not only on vote share but on turnout among constituencies—young renters and all minority communities—that are historically less reliably mobilized than older homeowners. A representative who becomes invisible, who is perceived as taking young and minority voter support for granted, or who loses credibility on the material issues (housing, jobs, healthcare) that motivate turnout will find that turnout suppression among the base is as effective a threat to the seat as opposition vote gain. The 31% Republican share is large enough that even a 5-percentage-point drop in Democratic turnout, combined with modest opposition gains, flips the seat in a competitive cycle." }
