          "Understand that the 66% Democratic result is not a Democratic base — it is a Latino coalition that voted Democratic in 2024, and retention depends on perception that the elected representative is actually improving material conditions for working families. Republicans should target Hispanic voter messaging on economic performance and wage growth; Democrats should ensure they are delivering visible constituent service and economic wins that justify continued Latino support.",
          "Mobilize the 63% Hispanic majority as the core constituency while building genuine relationships with the 26% white community (likely working-class itself at this income level) — the split result suggests both communities contain swing voters sensitive to economic messaging and local governance performance rather than inherited partisan loyalty.",
          "Address the under-representation in college attainment (19.4%) by making workforce development, vocational training, and apprenticeship pathways central to economic messaging — this is a community where good jobs without four-year degree requirements determine family economic security and are a credible policy domain for legislative action."
