          { name: "Water Security and Infrastructure Investment", tag: "lean-into", why: "Arizona's water challenges are a direct governance concern for affluent suburban homeowners and professionals whose property values and quality of life depend on reliable water access and infrastructure investment. At $107,418 median income in a state facing long-term water scarcity, this constituency expects state legislators to manage water policy competently and proactively. A candidate who can articulate a specific vision for water security—aquifer management, surface water allocation, infrastructure investment—demonstrates the governance competence this educated, affluent electorate demands and provides a concrete reason to support their candidacy beyond partisan identity." },
          { name: "Professional Minority Community Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "College-educated Asian, Hispanic, and Black professionals together represent 17.51% of AZ-SD-4—nearly one-fifth of the district and a potentially decisive swing constituency in a 52-48 race. These voters tend to prioritize education access, healthcare affordability, economic opportunity, and fiscal management more than cultural conservatism; they are capable of supporting Republican candidates who deliver on these priorities, but will vote Democratic if they perceive the Republican candidate as indifferent to their concerns or hostile on cultural dimensions. Treating professional minority communities as an afterthought or secondary constituency, rather than engaging them as core to the district, is a strategic error in a district this close and this educated." },
          { name: "Partisan Tribalism as a Campaign Foundation", tag: "avoid", why: "AZ-SD-4's 52-48 result in an affluent, college-educated suburban district reflects a constituency that actively rejects purely partisan reasoning as a basis for voting. At this income and education level, voters have the sophistication and economic security to vote against their party's candidate if they conclude he or she is incompetent or misaligned with their priorities. Any candidate who leads with partisan messaging rather than governance competence and concrete policy proposals is conceding the district to an opponent willing to address voters' actual concerns." },
          { name: "Treating a Four-Point Margin as a Stable Republican Base", tag: "avoid", why: "Werner's 52% win is not a mandate or a sign of a safely Republican district; it is a narrow plurality in a district where nearly half the voters actively chose someone else. Any incumbent or challenger who treats this margin as a floor rather than a warning about voter dissatisfaction—or who consolidates only the existing Republican coalition without actively addressing opposition concerns—is failing to account for the specific competitive dynamics that produced a 52-48 result in a district that should be comfortably Republican. The 48% opposition in an affluent suburban district is a strategic fact demanding active response, not a partisan aberration to be ignored." }
