          "AZ-SD-5 is a cost-of-living constituency with a Democratic majority built on Hispanic voter support, renter-majority economics, and a relatively young (36.2 median age), college-educated cohort (40.2%) that evaluates representatives on concrete delivery rather than partisan identity. The 56.6% renter rate is the single most important demographic signal: in a district where the majority of households lack housing security and are likely spending $1,400-1,800 monthly on rent out of a $70,012 annual income, housing policy is not a secondary issue but the primary material reality structuring constituents' economic lives. The 36.69% Hispanic population represents the electoral foundation of the 68% Democratic result and requires ongoing, year-round constituent service — not periodic engagement during election cycles. A representative who treats housing affordability, renter protections, and economic mobility pathways as foundational governance priorities commands the policy domain most directly relevant to this district's lived experience.",
